            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "REDWULF"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Excellent! Tastes just like the real thing. Instead of mixing up and then letting it sit for 4 hours, I just stirred into pulled pork and cooked in the crockpot for a few hours. Every last bite was infused with this wonderful vinegar sauce!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ARMY WIFE"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Awesome! Made it as is. I cooked a 4lb. pork sirloin roast all day in the crock-pot in a can of consomme. I cooked this sauce on the stove to dissolve the sugar. About an hour before serving I took out the meat and shredded it with 2 forks, tossing out the fat and grissle. Then I poured out the juices from the crock-pot, replaced the meat, and poured on the sauce and mixed it in real good. I kept it on low until time to eat. Yummy! Just like home! Serve with some grilled buttered texas toast!",
            "datePublished": "2009-04-11T17:24:45.533Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "jncaquel"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This immediately put at the top of our \"Make Again\" pile! It is fantastic. For a healthier option, I boiled and shredded 1 1/5 lb of chicken thighs (to give consistency of traditional BBQ) and 1 1/5 lb boneless chicken breast. Shredded and doubled this recipe. DELICIOUS! Even the hard to convince southern guys ate it right up and came back for 2nds!",
            "datePublished": "2012-06-02T17:27:13.080Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Amber Cox"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Pretty sure this recipe should use 1 tsp of salt and not 1 tbsp. It was much too salty.",
            "datePublished": "2018-02-25T01:15:37.727Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Soup Loving Nicole"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made two different sauces to go with my pulled pork and this one was by far our favorite. Simple, tangy, and right amount of heat. 1 Tablespoon of salt was too much though. 1-2 teaspoons would be perfect.",
            "datePublished": "2024-06-30T20:07:50.256Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "COURTNEY77700"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Wow as is! Except doubled and for the second split apple cidar with distilled. Made the night before and let is sit in mason jar. I did heat it up the next day so the pepper didn't sit so much. Wonderful.\n\nUpdate: I have made this many times at this point. The best that it was I made the following three changes. Cut the cider vinegar with distilled. 1/4 for reg serving. 1/2 cup if doubled. We make salsa here in the summer and can it for the winter. The tomato juice drained off the salsa is a great addition. About two tablespoons. Add levels of flavor. I cook on low for a few minutes or let sit for atleast a day.\n\nGuess I'll have to put my salsa recipe on here one day.",
            "datePublished": "2010-01-05T09:02:15.633Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "cookin' for two"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I am so glad to finally have this recipe!! I haved been hooked on this stuff for decades at the mercy of the bbq joints.\nI cooked a Boston butt in the crock pot all day, but only added salt, ground black pepper, about a tbsp of brown sugar and a cup of vinegar for cooking. I have a spice hater at the table, so I mixed the sauce when I started the roast and served it only as a side sauce.The pork was delish with just the mild vinegar, but goes insane good with the kick! Thank you for sharing this awesome favorite!!",
            "datePublished": "2011-07-23T21:09:15.600Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Kevin Dietzel"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great NC sauce. I was cooking a whole pork shoulder so I doubled the recipe and it came out a bit strong. I cut it with some hard cider and it came out perfect.",
            "datePublished": "2015-07-04T12:37:26.253Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Cook and a half"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is delicious! It makes the best pulled pork!!! I made this and then added it into the crock pot with some of the juices the meat was cooked in and added the meat again. I let it \"talk\" for about a half hour on high in the crockpot. Came out wonderful and tasty!",
            "datePublished": "2013-05-04T18:51:53.373Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Complexanomaly"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It was good. It was my first time making bbq. I didn't have a pork butt so I slow cooked country style ribs and shredded it. I changed it a little and might need to change it next time I make it. 1 cup of Apple cider vinegar was too much! It tasted too much apple cidery.....I put 1 tablespoon and a half of red pepper flakes and no cayenne pepper. it was nice and spicy. will make again with small changes.",
            "datePublished": "2014-03-23T14:32:41.887Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "vanillaripple"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made a SUGAR FREE version of this\nrecipe, great for all those watching their sugar. It turned out just as good as the original. I substituted 1 talespoon of Xylitol for the brown sugar and added 1 dropper full of Chocolate Liquid STEVIA. it was deliciously yummy!",
